WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.160
- Hey, I understand.

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:05.730
I mean, everybody's approach
to quarantine is different

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.500
and it's all relative in everybody's life.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.190
But I understand your
approach to quarantine

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:12.430
was less toilet paper and more booze.

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:13.800
Is that right? (laughing)

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:14.880
- I couldn't believe it.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.763
Well, like on day one of lockdown,

00:00:17.763 --> 00:00:21.080
everywhere in the world
people going to supermarkets

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.460
and stockpiling toilet,

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.730
or fighting over toilet rolls.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:26.590
How many do you need?
- I know.

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:29.060
- I just thought, that's ridiculous.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:32.917
I would give up my last toilet
roll for a glass of beer.

00:00:32.917 --> 00:00:35.200
(laughing)

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.150
If it's the end of the world,

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:41.450
I'd rather be drunk than
have lots of toilet rolls.

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:43.500
I just didn't get it.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.780
- We're the same person.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:47.680
I thought it was crazy, the
toilet paper crisis of 2020.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:50.040
I was like, how 'bout food?

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.320
How 'bout ramen noodles?
- I know.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:52.380
- Like something that'll last forever.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:53.500
- It was such an arbitrary thing.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:55.730
I've never, I'd never
thought about it before,

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:57.210
but it's such a...

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.749
But I mean, those people
who fighting on day one,

00:00:59.749 --> 00:01:00.880
they're the sort of people

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:02.670
that if there was a plane crash,

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:04.940
they'd start eating other passengers.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:07.133
I'd be going, "There's still sandwiches."

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:07.966
(laughing)

00:01:07.966 --> 00:01:09.690
"We've still got some,
don't eat this plane."

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:10.980
- Or crackers. (laughing)

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:12.271
- Yeah, yeah.

00:01:12.271 --> 00:01:13.830
No, put the leg down.

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:16.580
- Well something I love that
you've done in quarantine,

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:19.800
is there a badminton court at your house?

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.600
- Yes, yes.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.310
I thought we've got a garden,

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:26.993
and then we found a badminton
set we hadn't opened

00:01:26.993 --> 00:01:30.400
in one of those cupboards
that you do for like 10 years.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:31.510
We put it up.

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:35.150
Then we ordered some
spray chalk from Amazon

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:36.760
and we put out the markings,

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:38.930
and now we play badminton everyday.

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:42.362
Yeah, so that's really been great, yeah.

00:01:42.362 --> 00:01:43.195
- Amazing.

00:01:43.195 --> 00:01:45.400
Like who goes for, you don't play catch,

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.310
you don't do like a normal, that's just so

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:49.780
like farfetched of a sport. (laughing)

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:52.060
- It is, it's not a
sport that I would have,

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:53.650
you know, played in school in stuff.

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:56.849
Also, there's a lot of
bending over in badminton.

00:01:56.849 --> 00:01:59.440
Because with tennis I can do the thing

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:00.550
where I get the ball up.

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:03.740
But there's a lot of bending
over to pick the shuttlecock up

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:04.960
because it doesn't bounce.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:06.530
And I'm getting to that age now

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:09.170
where I make a noise when I bend over

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:10.215
and have to stand up.

00:02:10.215 --> 00:02:12.159
That (pained groaning).

00:02:12.159 --> 00:02:15.180
So (laughing) -
- I do that, too.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:18.353
I'm 38, whatever.
(laughing)

